  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a coral reef gains, loses, and redistributes calcium carbonate (CaCO₃). Overall, it shows the reef as a balance between carbonate production by calcification and carbonate loss through dissolution and erosion.\n\nCarbonate enters the reef system as CaCO₃ import and is added to the living surface layer by reef organisms that calcify. At the same time, several processes remove material. Dissolution occurs in seawater and within the reef framework, while biological erosion is driven by microborers inside the skeleton and by grazers such as parrotfish and sea urchins that physically break down coral. This erosion generates loose sediment or rubble.\n\nThe diagram also emphasizes internal recycling: eroded carbonate fragments are transported across the reef surface and can later infill spaces in the framework. Beneath the active surface layer, the solidified reef undergoes diagenetic processes that seem to stabilize or cement material, counteracting deeper dissolution and erosion to some extent.\n\nTaken together, the image presents the reef as a dynamic carbonate budget system, where growth depends on whether calcification and lithification outweigh dissolution, bioerosion, and export of CaCO₃ out of the reef.",
